# How do people choose? Putting it together
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Rational choice, behavioural, imperial, and norms: four ways of seeing When each works best"
next: {"title":"Test: how do people choose?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/105-test-how-do-people-choose.cast"}

## Settled what, contested why
sources:
    - id: kbrg1981
      title: Demand Curves for Animal Consumers
      authors: J. H. Kagel, R. C. Battalio, H. Rachlin, L. Green
      year: 1981
      finding: Both income-compensated and ordinary demand curves of laboratory animals slope downward.
    - id: becker1962
      title: Irrational Behavior and Economic Theory
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1962
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 70(1): even households choosing at random within their budgets produce downward-sloping market demand curves, because a price rise shrinks the set of affordable bundles.'
    - id: ms2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: 'QJE 116(4): participation at 3–15 months of tenure was 37% under opt-in and 86% under automatic enrolment.'
    - id: jps2006
      title: Household Expenditure and the Income Tax Rebates of 2001
      authors: D. S. Johnson, J. A. Parker, N. S. Souleles
      year: 2006
      finding: 'AER 96(5): households spent roughly 20–40% of the 2001 rebates on nondurable goods in the three-month period they arrived.'
    - id: psjm2013
      title: Consumer Spending and the Economic Stimulus Payments of 2008
      authors: J. A. Parker, N. S. Souleles, D. S. Johnson, R. McClelland
      year: 2013
      finding: 'AER 103(6): about 12–30% of the 2008 payments spent on nondurables within three months.'
    - id: lps
      title: Heuristic Thinking and Limited Attention in the Car Market
      authors: N. Lacetera, D. G. Pope, J. R. Sydnor
      year: 2012
      finding: 'AER 102(5): used-car auction prices drop discontinuously at each 10,000-mile odometer threshold.'
    - id: salganik2006
      title: Experimental Study of Inequality and Unpredictability in an Artificial Cultural Market
      authors: Salganik, M. J., Dodds, P. S., Watts, D. J.
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Science 311(5762): 854–856. 14,341 participants downloaded 48 previously unknown songs, either independently or seeing earlier downloads in one of eight separate ''worlds''; social influence raised both the inequality and the unpredictability of success.'
      doi: 10.1126/science.1121066

