    card title "Settled what, contested why"

How do people choose? This block asked it for thirty-odd lectures. So put its verdicts on one board: what people do, and why.
    draw h_what rule h_why divider

B: Row one, the oldest: when a  (@draw w1@)price rises, people buy less of it. About as close to consensus as (@draw s1@) economics gets.

Row two: the option you get (@draw w2@) for doing nothing tends to stick. Make a pension opt-out instead of opt-in, and enrolment jump (@draw s2@)s. Consensus.

B: Row three: when a tax reb (@draw w3@)ate lands, people spend a real chunk of it within months, not spread over a lifetime. Con (@draw s3@)sensus again.

And row four: buyer (@draw w4@)s react to the first digit. Used cars sell for less just past each ten-thousand-mile mark. Mo (@draw s4@)stly agreed.

B: Row five, the newe (@draw w5@)st: crowds snowball. Hits, queues, full restaurants: early choices pull later ones along. Mo (@draw s5@)stly agreed.

B: Now the other column. (@highlight h_why@) Why do people do these things? What verdict do you think the block kept reaching?

    quiz "The block's verdict on WHY people do these things, row after row?" judge false voice b
        right: Noted. The board answers row by row.
        * Consensus
        * Mostly agreed
        * Contested

Row one: do b (@draw y1@)uyers really optimise, or does a shrinking budget do the work? C (@draw c1@)ontested.

B: Inert (@draw y2@)ia, or a default read as advice?  (@draw c2@)Contested. (@draw y3@) Impatient, or simply short of cash? Co (@draw c3@)ntested.

A cleve (@draw y4@)r shortcut, or a bias? Contested too. And the (@draw c4@) crowd: sound infere (@draw y5@)nce from what others know, or plain conformity?  (@draw c5@)Contested.

B: So that's the block in one pattern. What people do is mostl (@highlight whycol effect box@)y settled. Why they do it is where the fight is.

An (@erase board@)d the fight has four sides: ra (@draw l_rc@)tional choice, behavi (@draw l_be@)oural economics, Beck (@draw l_im@)er's imperial reach, an (@draw l_no@)d norms.

    pause 0.5

## One machine, four lenses
sources:
    - id: stiglerbecker1977
      title: De Gustibus Non Est Disputandum
      authors: George J. Stigler, Gary S. Becker
      year: 1977
      finding: 'AER 67(2): treat tastes as stable and alike across people; explain differences in behaviour by prices and incomes.'
    - id: becker1968
      title: 'Crime and Punishment: An Economic Approach'
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1968
      finding: 'JPE 76(2): an offence is a choice weighed against the expected punishment, which acts as a price.'
    - id: akerlofkranton2000
      title: Economics and Identity
      authors: George A. Akerlof, Rachel E. Kranton
      year: 2000
      finding: 'QJE 115(3): a person''s identity, and the norms of the category they belong to, enter the utility function.'
    - id: tk1992
      title: Advances in Prospect Theory
      authors: Amos Tversky, Daniel Kahneman
      year: 1992
      finding: Losses weigh more than equal gains (median λ = 2.25 in their lab study).
    - id: laibson1997
      title: Golden Eggs and Hyperbolic Discounting
      authors: David Laibson
      year: 1997
      finding: 'QJE 112(2): the β–δ model of present bias.'

    box n_want "Wants (U)" x 200 y 510 width 290 height 110 radius 12 icon heart font_size 36 hidden true
