    cards sortq bins ["Wants","Prices","Choosing"] check each hidden true
        * "Rival's shirt, free" bin Wants
        Choosing: Trial turns paid
        Prices: More store guards
        Wants: Gift for gift
        Choosing: Gym auto-renews
        Prices: Night bus fare doubles
    text t_hap "U ranks choices; ≠ happiness" #8a5fa8 x 200 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "One machine, four lenses"

Four sides, and they all fight over the  (@draw n_out@)why. So where exactly do they part ways?

B: Start from the machine they share. Wants go in here: (@draw n_want@) U, what you like, ranked best to worst. Not the sa (@draw t_hap@)me as happiness: people give up some happiness for purpose or status.

    erase t_hap

B: Prices and a bud (@draw n_price@)get go in here: what you can afford.

B: And in the midd (@draw n_choose@)le, the choosing: pick the bes (@draw a1 a2 a3 parallel true@)t thing you can afford. That's rational (@draw t_rc@) choice, whole.

And Becker's i (@highlight n_price@)mperialism? A different machine?

B: Same machine, wider price box. A prison t (@draw t_im@)erm is a price. So are a child's hours, and the slim chance your vote counts.

Then behavioural economics rewir (@draw t_be@)es the (@highlight n_choose@) middle: losses loom larger, now beats later, the default wins, and some prices go unseen.

B: And norms rewrite the w (@draw t_no@)ants (@highlight n_want@): fairness, what others expect, who you are, where you grew up.

So they're not four theories of everything. Each ke (@highlight tags@)eps most of the machine and opens one box.

B: Your turn. Six new cases: which b (@hide machine@)ox does each one poke?

    draw sortq

    ask "Which box does each case poke? Sort the six cards." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        right: Wants: the shirt and the gift. Choosing: the trial and the gym renewal. Prices: the guards and the bus fare.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try. Here: shirt and gift poke what people want; the trial and the gym renewal, how they pick; the guards and the bus fare, the price.

    show machine

That's why the four can argue at a (@highlight boxes@)ll: they share every box but one.

    pause 0.5

## When each works best
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10.8]}
sources:
    - id: becker1962
      title: Irrational Behavior and Economic Theory
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1962
      finding: 'JPE 70(1): households choosing at random within their budgets still produce downward-sloping market demand, because a higher price shrinks the affordable set.'
    - id: ms2001
      title: 'The Power of Suggestion: Inertia in 401(k) Participation and Savings Behavior'
      authors: Brigitte C. Madrian, Dennis F. Shea
      year: 2001
      finding: Participation 37% under opt-in vs 86% under automatic enrolment.
    - id: dvm2006
      title: Paying Not to Go to the Gym
      authors: Stefano DellaVigna, Ulrike Malmendier
      year: 2006
      finding: 'AER 96(3): members on flat monthly fees paid more per visit than the per-visit price, consistent with overconfident present bias.'
    - id: ts2008
      title: 'Nudge: Improving Decisions about Health, Wealth, and Happiness'
      authors: Richard H. Thaler, Cass R. Sunstein
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Chapter ''When do we need a nudge?'': choices that are difficult, infrequent, with delayed or poor feedback, and with benefits and costs far apart in time.'
    - id: azar2020
      title: The Economics of Tipping
      authors: Ofer H. Azar
      year: 2020
      finding: Tipping amounts to tens of billions of dollars a year in the US.
    - id: camerer2003
      title: Behavioral Game Theory
      authors: Colin F. Camerer
      year: 2003
      finding: Ultimatum offers average about 40% of the pie; offers below 20% are rejected about half the time.
    - id: ggl2008
      title: 'Social Pressure and Voter Turnout: Evidence from a Large-Scale Field Experiment'
      authors: Alan S. Gerber, Donald P. Green, Christopher W. Larimer
      year: 2008
      finding: 'APSR 102(1): telling households their turnout would be shown to neighbours raised turnout by about 8 percentage points.'
    - id: list2003
      title: Does Market Experience Eliminate Market Anomalies?
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2003
      finding: 'QJE 118(1): dealers and experienced traders show little endowment effect; inexperienced collectors a large one.'

