    card title "When each works best"

If each l (@draw ax bot mid ceil@)ens opens one box, when should you reach for which? Two questions sort most cases.

B: First, along  (@draw lab_x@)here: how often do you make this choice, and how fast do you learn it went wrong? Sec (@draw lab_y@)ond, up here: is anyone watching?

Everyday an (@draw r_rc z_rc@)d private, down here: groceries, petrol, (@draw p_cof p_cof_l@) coffee. Rational choice does well, and price changes show it.

B: Even sloppy shoppers help it. Becker s (@draw rnd@)howed that buyers who chose at random would still trace a demand curve that slopes down.

    erase rnd

    fade ax lab_x lab_y r_rc r_be r_no z_rc z_be z_no p_cof p_cof_l to 0.12 duration 0.3

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own

@math

    erase mathq

Say a buyer picks a point on her budget line at random: any amount of the good from zero to I over P.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1 calc_line_2

B: On average s (@draw calc_line_3@)he buys half the most she could: I over two P.

Double the price  (@draw calc_line_4@)and that average halves. A downward slope, with no optimising at all.

    erase calc

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax lab_x lab_y r_rc r_be r_no z_rc z_be z_no p_cof p_cof_l to 1

Rare and sl (@draw r_be z_be@)ow to pay back, over here: a pens (@draw p_pen p_pen_l@)ion, a gym contract.  (@draw p_gym p_gym_l@)That's where defaults and present bias bite.

B: And up top (@draw r_no z_no@), anything others see: the tip, the fair (@draw p_tip p_tip_l@) split, the vot (@draw p_split p_split_l@)e. Norms do much of  (@draw p_vote p_vote_l@)the work there.

B: Not everything up here is a norm, though. Joining a queue can be plain inference fr (@draw p_q p_q_l@)om what others seem to know, until it becomes a cascade.

A new case: choosing a mortgage, once or twice in a life. My guess is the ratio (@highlight z_rc effect circle@)nal corner. The stakes are huge, so people take care.

    ask "Where does choosing a mortgage land? Tap a zone." intro "Where would you put it?" choose ["z_rc","z_be","z_no"] answer z_be stage own voice b
        right: Behavioural: rare, complex, and the cost shows up years later. Big stakes don't buy practice.
        wrong: How often do people choose a mortgage, and when do they learn it was a good one? Rarely, and late: here, behavioural.

    draw p_mort p_mort_l

Now change one thing. Hand the sam (@draw a_brk@)e choice to a broker who arranges two hundre (@draw p_brk p_brk_l@)d mortgages a year.

B: Same choice, now practised, with feedback, an (@highlight p_brk effect pulse@)d it slides right. Experienced card dealers lost the endowment effect the same way.

So the map doesn't say which lens is true. It s (@highlight lab_x lab_y@)ays which one the situation calls for.

    pause 0.5

## Patchwork or theory?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: You'd pass the block's test already.
    good: Ask how often, and who's watching.
    poor: 'Back to the map: practice, and who is watching.'
    none: The map will keep.
sources:
    - id: tk1992
      title: Advances in Prospect Theory
      authors: Amos Tversky, Daniel Kahneman
      year: 1992
      finding: Median loss aversion λ = 2.25 in a laboratory study.
    - id: laibson1997
      title: Golden Eggs and Hyperbolic Discounting
      authors: David Laibson
      year: 1997
      finding: 'QJE 112(2): the β–δ model of present bias.'
    - id: ro1968
      title: A Theory of the Calculus of Voting
      authors: William H. Riker, Peter C. Ordeshook
      year: 1968
      finding: 'APSR 62(1): a citizen votes if pB − C + D > 0, D the satisfaction of doing one''s civic duty.'
    - id: ak2000
      title: Economics and Identity
      authors: George A. Akerlof, Rachel E. Kranton
      year: 2000
      finding: 'QJE 115(3): identity and the norms of one''s social category enter the utility function.'
    - id: sb1977
      title: De Gustibus Non Est Disputandum
      authors: George J. Stigler, Gary S. Becker
      year: 1977
      finding: 'AER 67(2): treat tastes as stable over time and similar among people; explain behaviour by prices and incomes.'
    - id: henrich2001
      title: 'In Search of Homo Economicus: Behavioral Experiments in 15 Small-Scale Societies'
      authors: J. Henrich, R. Boyd, S. Bowles, C. Camerer, E. Fehr, H. Gintis, R. McElreath
      year: 2001
      finding: 'AER 91(2): mean ultimatum offers across the 15 societies ranged from 26% to 58%.'
    - id: list2003
      title: Does Market Experience Eliminate Market Anomalies?
      authors: John A. List
      year: 2003
      finding: 'QJE 118(1): experienced traders show little endowment effect.'
    - id: gcg2008
      title: 'A Room with a Viewpoint: Using Social Norms to Motivate Environmental Conservation in Hotels'
      authors: Noah J. Goldstein, Robert B. Cialdini, Vladas Griskevicius
      year: 2008
      finding: 'J. Consumer Research 35(3): a sign saying most guests reuse their towels raised reuse more than the standard environmental appeal.'

