    card title "Patchwork or theory?"

B: Here's my worry with that tidy map. Ev (@draw u@)ery time a choice misbehaved, the utility line got a patc (@draw pa1@)h: a λ for losses, a  (@draw pa2@)β for now.

A taste for duty  (@draw pa3@)to explain the vote. An identity term for  (@draw pa4@)the shirt you won't wear.

B: A patch measured once and then predicting somewhere new is (@highlight patches@) science. A patch for every surprise is a label.

Becker and Stigler wanted the opposite disciplin (@draw sb@)e: assume everyone's tastes are st (@fade patches to 0.3@)able and alike, and explain every difference by prices.

B: That forbids a lot, and it's tested  (@highlight sb@)hard: ultimatum offers swing widely across fifteen small societies, and whether different prices or different tastes explain it is still argued.

    erase u patches sb

B: And the verdicts are firmest (@draw ev1@) where lab, field and natural data agree, as with defaults. They're shakiest where (@draw ev2@) only the lab speaks: generous in the lab, nothing shared at the bus stop.

    erase ev1 ev2

So  (@draw rank@)how sure are we? Rank these claims from least sure to most.

    ask "Rank the four claims from least sure to most sure." on rank stage own store k
        right: Demand slopes down at the top, one grand theory at the bottom: you got {k}.
        wrong: Which claims rest on many studies, and which on how far one theory reaches? Here: least sure, one grand theory; then crime, family and voting; then practice; most sure, demand slopes down.

    erase rank

B: That deman (@draw c1 s1@)d slopes down: consensus. That practice an (@draw c2@)d feedback shrink biases in markets: most (@draw s2@)ly agreed.

That crime,  (@draw c3@)marriage and voting are mainly rational ch (@draw s3@)oices: contested.  (@draw c4@)And one theory that holds all four lenses: (@draw s4@) open.

    quiz "People pay for phone plans they rarely use. An economist adds a 'taste for flexibility' that fits the data exactly. What would make it more than a relabel?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: A prediction somewhere new: a taste measured once must show up again in a choice it wasn't built for.
        wrong: Any label fits the data it was made from: here, a prediction somewhere new.
        + It predicts another choice it wasn't fitted to
        * It fits these data perfectly
        * It keeps the buyer rational

    quiz "A hotel tries two signs: 'Help save the environment' and 'Most guests in this hotel reuse their towels'. Which lens predicts the second works better?" voice b
        right: Norms: what others do shapes what you want, and in a real hotel field test the 'most guests' sign won.
        wrong: The sign changes no price and no default, only what others are said to do: here, norms.
        + Norms
        * Rational choice
        * Imperial

    quiz "Which would the rational-choice model predict best?"
        right: The supermarket: many buyers, every week, with fast feedback, and averages that slope down even when some shoppers err.
        wrong: Look for a choice made often, by many, with fast feedback: here, the supermarket.
        + A supermarket's weekly sales after a price cut
        * One couple's decision to divorce
        * A first-time buyer's mortgage pick

B: So how d (@erase ladder@)o people choose? Much as the machine says when the choice is eve (@draw f1@)ryday and unwatched; by shortcut (@draw f2@)s when it's rare; by the group's (@draw f3@) rules when others look.

And economics came into this block as a method for choo (@draw fin@)sing, and leaves it as a method that knows where it holds, and where it needs help.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
